# `<Your name>`'s workspace

> Always-loaded context that Claude Code reads at session start. Keep it short — every line costs tokens on every turn.

## What this is

A brief description of the working directory. Example:

> Multi-project workspace with personal admin, a side project, health records, and a writing project. Each subdirectory has its own `CLAUDE.md` and `CONTEXT.md`.

## Working principles

- **Simplicity first** — prefer editing existing files; no new abstractions without clear need.
- **Minimal impact** — only touch what the task requires.
- **Ask before destructive actions** — delete, force-push, mass-rewrite all need confirmation.
- **Honest communication** — no performative agreement, no empty affirmations.

## Grounding and calibration

For any load-bearing claim about workspace state — a file's contents, a path, a config flag, a registry value, a status (`done` / `shipped`), a line number — read the source and cite `path:line` before asserting it. "Not found in `<file>`" beats a guess.

Tag a genuinely uncertain load-bearing claim `[unverified]` rather than stating it flat. Scope this to workspace-state claims: do not over-tag general reasoning or well-known facts, or the marker stops carrying information.

This extends the `researcher` role's source discipline (otherwise active only inside a research task) to ordinary answers. See [`roles/researcher.md`](roles/researcher.md) for the two-axis grading scheme it inherits.

## Self-improvement loop

- After any user correction, append a rule to `tasks/lessons.md` **before** continuing other work.
- At session start, read `tasks/lessons.md` and apply its rules for the duration of the session.

## Task management

1. Write the plan to `tasks/todo.md` with checkable items.
2. Verify the plan with the user before implementing.
3. Mark items complete as you go.
4. Append a review section on completion.

## Command shortcuts

Verbal phrases that map directly to a destination — go there without asking.

| Phrase | Target |
|---|---|
| "add to tasks" / "add to the list" | `tasks/To Do Notes.md` |
| "add a lesson" | `tasks/lessons.md` (prepend entry) |
| "orient" / "get me up to speed" | run the `orient` skill |
| "wrap" / "close out" | run the `wrap` skill |

Rule: when a new shortcut emerges, ask once then add it to this table — so the shortcut works next time.

## Credentials

**Iron Law: credentials never live in files.** Passwords, API keys, tokens, recovery codes all live in your password manager. This file references credentials only by their password-manager item name, never their value.

If the user pastes a credential into chat (happens occasionally), refuse to save it, explain why, and recommend regeneration because the transcript has already leaked it.

## Token discipline

Context cost is size times steps. The model API is stateless, so the whole transcript is re-sent on every inference step — every tool call, not only every user message — and a token's cost is its size times the steps remaining after it enters. Manage both multipliers:

- **Read in a subagent.** Bulk or exploratory reading goes to an isolated agent that returns a short summary; its context never enters the caller. Give it a return budget: write detail to a file, return a summary plus the path.
- **Read ranges, not whole files**, and defer a big read to the step that needs it. Late is cheaper.
- **Batch independent tool calls into one step.** Sequential calls each re-read the whole context; batched calls re-read it once. Sequence only when a later call depends on an earlier result.
- **Mind the cache clock.** A cache re-write bills the whole context at a premium. What forces one is resuming after an idle gap longer than the cache lifetime, a mid-session model switch, or compaction, not editing a standing instruction or memory file (the loaded copy is captured at session start and never re-sent, so those edits take effect next session and their timing is free). Wrap and clear before a break that will outlive the cache; change models only in a fresh session. `scripts/cache_write_scan.py` attributes a transcript's cache writes so the rule stays measured.
- **Reuse a spawned worker while its cache is warm.** When follow-up work shares an agent's context, continue that agent with a message rather than dispatching a fresh one: its accumulated context re-serves at the cached-read rate, but only within the provider's short subagent cache TTL, so send the continuation promptly.
- **Compact on purpose** at high context — it shortens the prefix every remaining step re-reads. Pair it with a state dump so nothing load-bearing is lost.
